@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+---
+type: adr
+status: stable
+entities: [Post]
+related:
+  - app/Http/Controllers/PostController.ts
+  - app/Http/Resources/PostResource.ts
+  - app/Models/Post.ts
+  - app/Support/slug.ts
+  - routes/web.ts
+generated: { by: "human:Urata Daiki", at: 2026-08-18T04:42:51.295Z }
+---
+
+# Address posts by slug
+
+## Context
+
+Posts were addressed by their database id (`/posts/42`). Readers and
+search engines get nothing from the number, and links already published
+against those ids must not break.
+
+## Decision
+
+Posts carry a `slug` column: `NOT NULL UNIQUE`, derived from the title at
+creation by `slugify()` in `app/Support/slug.ts` (lowercase ASCII letters
+and digits, words joined by a single `-`). `Post.uniqueSlugFor()` resolves
+collisions by appending the next free number — `hello-world`,
+`hello-world-2`, `hello-world-3`.
+
+The slug is **frozen after creation**: it is absent from `Post.fillable`
+and never recomputed on update, so retitling a post does not move its
+address.
+
+`GET /posts/:slug` serves the post page. The same route also answers the
+legacy id addresses: a purely numeric segment is looked up as an id and,
+when it matches, answered with a **301** to `/posts/<slug>`. Two routes
+cannot do this — `/posts/:id` and `/posts/:slug` are the same pattern, so
+the first registered would swallow every request. A numeric segment that
+matches no id falls through to a slug lookup before 404ing.
+
+Edit, update and delete stay id-based (`/posts/:id/edit`, `PUT /posts/:id`,
+`DELETE /posts/:id`): they are authenticated author-only actions with no
+SEO value, and keeping them on the primary key avoids a second lookup path.
+
+Posts created before this change were backfilled with `post-<id>` by the
+`add_post_slug` migration — unique and well-formed, without inventing a
+title-derived address retroactively.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Post URLs read as their titles, and `PostResource` exposes `slug`, so
+  every page links through it.
+- Old links keep working permanently, at the cost of one extra lookup for
+  numeric segments.
+- A post whose slug is itself numeric would be shadowed by the legacy id
+  branch. `slugify()` can produce such a slug (title `"2026"`), so this is
+  possible but rare; the id branch wins deliberately, since those are the
+  addresses already published.
+- Slug assignment reads the slug family and then inserts, so two
+  simultaneous creates of the same title can race. The `UNIQUE` constraint
+  is the backstop: the loser's insert fails rather than duplicating an
+  address.
